        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"Every Tuesday, Martin put a blue cup beside the library kettle. The other cups were white, with the council crest fading in the wash.\\n\\n“Who’s that one for?” asked Leena, on her first afternoon.\\n\\n“Mrs Vale. She brings the newspapers back.”\\n\\n“I thought newspapers stayed here.”\\n\\n“They do now.”\\n\\nAt eleven, a woman in a green coat appeared carrying a bundle tied with string. Martin filled the blue cup without asking.\\n\\nMrs Vale sat near the radiator and untied the bundle. Inside were last week’s papers, each folded to the crossword.\\n\\n“Seven down was wrong,” she said.\\n\\n“The clue or your answer?”\\n\\nShe gave him a look.\\n\\nIn November, the council removed the kettle. A notice explained that refreshments were available from the machine in the entrance hall.\\n\\nMrs Vale studied the buttons.\\n\\n“It wants a pound.”\\n\\n“Yes,” said Leena.\\n\\n“For that little cup.”\\n\\n“Yes.”\\n\\nShe returned to the radiator with her coat still buttoned.\\n\\nThe following Tuesday, Leena brought a flask. She poured tea into the blue cup beneath the desk, beside the returned books.\\n\\nMartin watched her.\\n\\n“That’s your own tea.”\\n\\n“I made too much.”\\n\\nBy January, there were three flasks under the desk. Nobody discussed them.\\n\\nOne Tuesday, Mrs Vale arrived without the newspapers.\\n\\n“Forgotten something?” Martin asked.\\n\\n“Apparently.”\\n\\nShe stood with her hands resting on her empty shopping bag.\\n\\nLeena fetched the blue cup.\\n\\n“We’ve got today’s,” she said, pulling out a chair.\\n\\nMrs Vale took off her coat. While the tea cooled, Martin found a pencil and rubbed out the answer he had written at seven down.\"\n}",
